\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8}{\ignorespaces Empirical Contrast between Official Reverse-JS and Corrected Standard-JS across Regularization Scales $\lambda \in [0.01, 1.0]$ on Kelmarsh and Penmanshiel (Multi-seed means and standard deviations). (a) Mean gradient $L_2$ norm: the reverse-argument JS form induces substantial gradient amplification at $\lambda = 1.0$ ($2.20\times $ on Kelmarsh, $2.72\times $ on Penmanshiel) due to the $-\frac  {P_{\text  {ref}, i}}{4 P_{\text  {pred}, i}}$ inverse-density singularity ($\mathcal  {O}(1/\epsilon )$ in Equation~\eqref  {eq:reverse_js_gradient}), whereas for Corrected Standard-JS the signed partial derivative possesses a finite upper bound ($\frac  {1}{2}\ln 2$) while exhibiting only mild logarithmic divergence ($\mathcal  {O}(|\log \epsilon |)$) as $P_{\text  {pred}, i} \to 0$. (b) Operational dispatch cost ($r=2$): Corrected Standard-JS prevents premature performance degradation at weak $\lambda =0.01$, while excessive regularization degrades dispatch across both variants.}}{19}{figure.caption.33}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:pct_contrast}{{8}{19}{Empirical Contrast between Official Reverse-JS and Corrected Standard-JS across Regularization Scales $\lambda \in [0.01, 1.0]$ on Kelmarsh and Penmanshiel (Multi-seed means and standard deviations). (a) Mean gradient $L_2$ norm: the reverse-argument JS form induces substantial gradient amplification at $\lambda = 1.0$ ($2.20\times $ on Kelmarsh, $2.72\times $ on Penmanshiel) due to the $-\frac {P_{\text {ref}, i}}{4 P_{\text {pred}, i}}$ inverse-density singularity ($\mathcal {O}(1/\epsilon )$ in Equation~\eqref {eq:reverse_js_gradient}), whereas for Corrected Standard-JS the signed partial derivative possesses a finite upper bound ($\frac {1}{2}\ln 2$) while exhibiting only mild logarithmic divergence ($\mathcal {O}(|\log \epsilon |)$) as $P_{\text {pred}, i} \to 0$. (b) Operational dispatch cost ($r=2$): Corrected Standard-JS prevents premature performance degradation at weak $\lambda =0.01$, while excessive regularization degrades dispatch across both variants}{figure.caption.33}{}}
